Back to Modeling the Material Flow
Active and Passive Objects
Mobile and stationary material flow objects are the basic items you use when you create a simulation
model.
• The mobile material flow objects Part, Container and Transporter represent the physical or logical
parts moving through the model, i.e., your installation. These mobile objects (MUs) require active and
passive material flow objects to process and transport them.
• The Source creates mobile objects at the beginning of the flow of materials through your plant. The
Drain removes the MUs from the plant after the material flow objects processed them.
Active and Passive Objects
10-152 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• The FluidSource creates fluids at the beginning of the flow of materials through your plant. The
FluidDrain removes the fluids from the plant after the fluid objects processed them.
• The active material flow objects Drain, Station, ParallelStation, AssemblyStation, DismantleStation,
Conveyor, Sorter, PlaceBuffer, and Buffer receive MUs, and process them for a certain time. They then
attempt to actively transfer the MUs to the next object along material flow connections, symbolized
by the Connector, using the push-block principle. They represent work stations in a factory, such as a
lathe or a drilling station, etc. They only differ in the number of MUs they can process at the same time,
a single one or several, and in the way they process them, in series or in parallel. Basically, the Conveyor
resembles a conveyor system transporting MUs at a given speed over a given distance.
• The active fluid objects FluidDrain, Tank, Mixer, Portioner, and DePortioner receive fluids, and process
them for a certain time. They then attempt to actively transfer the fluids to the next object along
material flow connections, symbolized by the Connector, using the push-block principle.
• The passive material flow objects Store, Track, and TwoLaneTrack do not automatically transfer MUs. A
MU remains in the Store until it is removed, for example by using a Method. You can only use the Track
and the TwoLaneTrack in a meaningful way in conjunction with the Transporter, which drives along the
Track/TwoLaneTrack with the speed you set. Model diverging strategies and converging strategies with
the FlowControl.
• The passive fluid objects Pipe and PatchMatrix transport the fluids to their respective successor in the
flow of materials.
We also distinguish between active and passive MUs:
• Active MUs are objects that can move by themselves, such as the Transporter, which can be self-
propelled, and the Worker, who represents a human.
• Passive MUs, such as the Part and the Container, are objects that are transported from material flow
object to material flow object, which process them. The Part represents any workpiece that is produced
and processed. The Container represents any type of receptacle, such as a storage bin or a pallet.
In addition, we differentiate between:
• Point-oriented objects in which the MUs are located on a fixed processing place. For the Source,
the Drain, the Station, the ParallelStation, the AssemblyStation, the DismantleStation, the Sorter, the
PlaceBuffer, the Buffer, the FluidSource, the FluidDrain, the Tank, the Mixer, the ContinuousMixer, the
Portioner, the DePortioner, and the PatchMatrix, their own real length and their dimension as well as
the length and the dimension of the MUs, which transfer onto them, are irrelevant for the simulation.
• Length-oriented objects whose own real length and dimension as well as the length and the
dimension of the MUs, which transfer onto them, are used during the simulation. Length-oriented
objects are the Conveyor, the Track, the TwoLaneTrack, the FootPath, the Pipe,the Container, and the
Transporter.
As opposed to the regular length-oriented objects, the Turntable consists of a single straight segment,
not of a sequence of straight and curved segments. The Turnplate consists of a straight segment that
sets the diameter of the Turnplate. The AngularConverter consists of two straight segments, not of a
sequence of straight and curved segments. The Converter consists of a single straight segment.
The length-oriented objects are called extrusion objects in 3D.
Active and Passive Objects
