Producing Parts with the Source
Produce Parts with the Source
To create the MUs, which the material flow objects in your simulation model handle, we use the object
Source. It might, for example, represent the receiving department of your installation that introduces parts
produced at another location into the plant. Or it can be a machine, which produces the parts that the
other stations process.
To remove these parts from the factory to model, for example, the shipping department, we use the object
Drain 
 .
You can insert the Source 
  into your simulation model from the folder MaterialFlow in the Class Library
or from the toolbar Material Flow in the Toolbox.
You can:
• Select How the Source Proceeds, When it Cannot Produce MUs
• Produce Parts According to a Delivery Table
• Produce Parts During an Interval Which You Define
• Produce the Number of Parts You Need
• Produce Parts Using a Trigger Object
Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
Compare Check How Many Parts Were Introduced into the Plant
Compare Remove Parts from the Installation with the Drain
Producing Parts with the Source
10-154 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model the Flow of Materials, Basics
Select How the Source Proceeds, When it Cannot Produce MUs
Select how the Source proceeds when it cannot create the MUs at the Time of Creation that you entered
because it cannot move the parts on to its successor. This is the case when it fails, when it is paused, or
when it is blocked.
• Select the Operating Mode > Blocking 
 , so that the Source remembers the time at which
it was supposed to produce the next MU but could not. It then produces the following MU at the next
possible point in time, i.e., if the MU, which blocked it, has moved on.
• Clear Blocking, so that the Source creates another MU exclusively at the time of creation you entered.
Note
When the Source is temporarily not operational because it is Failed, Paused, or Blocked, the times
of creation may shift, if you select Blocking. Then, the settings for the times of creation cannot be
realized.
Back to Produce Parts with the Source
Produce Parts According to a Delivery Table
For the installation you are modeling you will, most likely, use a Delivery Table that contains the parts that
a machine produces, parts provided by other departments within the same plant, or parts delivered from
other plants.
Most of the time the delivery table you receive from another department will be a Microsoft Excel file
containing, among others, the name of the part, its amount, its arrival time, etc. You will then open this
Excel (*.xls) file in a Plant Simulation table 
  and save it there.
Select How the Source Proceeds, When it Cannot Produce MUs
Plant Simulation Help 10-155
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To produce MUs according to the time, the type, and the number of parts you typed into a Delivery Table:
• Select Time of Creation > Delivery Table.
• Click 
  and select the delivery table you would like to use in the dialog Select Object. Or
Drag your delivery table over the text box Table and drop it there.
• Click OK.
Next to information about the name and number of the produced parts, the Delivery Table may also
contain values of their attributes. The Delivery Table has five columns.
Note
Instead of the data type time for the Delivery Time you can also use the data types date, dateTime, or
real. When you use date or dateTime, the start time of the simulation has to be before the time you type
in.
Each row of the Delivery Table defines a single order for producing MUs:
• Type the time at which the Source produces the MUs into the column Delivery Time.
• Type the class of the MU into the column MU. You can also use drag-and-drop of the MU class to
accomplish this.
• Type the number of MUs to be produced into the column Number.
Note
When you type 0 as Number into a row, Plant Simulation does take the specified interval for the next
creating cycle into account. This means that Plant Simulation does not skip this row, but does not
produce a part during this cycle.
Produce Parts According to a Delivery Table
10-156 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Type a name for the MUs to be produced into the column Name.
Note
You have to type in the Delivery Time and the MU.
If you do not type in a Number, Plant Simulation produces a single MU.
If you do not type in a Name, the MUs, which the Source produces, use the name of their class.
• In addition, you can, but you do not have to type the name of a subtable into the column Attributes.
Type the attributes whose value the Source sets and the user-defined attributes that it creates, when it
produces the MUs, into that subtable.
To open the subtable, do one of the following:
◦ Double-click the cell containing the subtable, properties in the column Attributes in the example
above.
◦ Click into the cell and press F2.
◦ Right-click into the cell and select Open Object.
Type the name of a built-in attribute into the cells below column 1 of the attribute table. Type the value
you want to assign to this attribute into one of the cells to the right with the respective data type. Or
Type the name of a user-defined attribute into the cells below column 1 of the table. If an attribute
with that name does not exist yet, Plant Simulation creates it. Type the value you want to assign to this
attribute into the cell to the right, which has the corresponding data type.
Produce Parts According to a Delivery Table
Plant Simulation Help 10-157
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
Plant Simulation assigns the data type of the column containing the value of the attribute to the data
type of the attribute itself. Plant Simulation assigns the value you type into the cell to the generated
attribute.
