Producing and Processing Parts with a Work Plan
Produce and Process Parts with a Work Plan
A work plan or operations plan determines how a product is produced. It lists the various production steps
in the sequence in which they are executed.
Producing and Processing Parts with a Work Plan
10-170 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
We neither specify the cost center, to which each of the individual production steps is charged in Plant
Simulation, nor the allowed time for each step. This you can do in Process Designer.
For simplicity’s sake each operation can only be executed by a single station in our example. Our work
plan thus is described by a sequence of stations. The Source named Receiving holds position 0 in the work
plan, the station Milling position 1, the stations Drilling_A or Drilling_B position 2 and the station Packing
position 3.
To facilitate quick and easy modeling:
• We create the work plan in a DataTable 
  and insert it into the Frame, in which we create our
simulation model. We enter the set-up times of the stations and the processing times of the different
parts into sub-tables of the work plan.
• We produce two different types of parts with a Source 
  using a sequence table. The parts need two
user-defined attributes. One sets the name of the part. The other sets the position of the part in the
work plan.
• We program a Method 
 , i.e., an Exit Control, which enables the processing station to find the next
station in the sequence of operations after each processing step and moves the part on to this station.
We insert this Method, which all stations access, into the Frame.
Produce and Process Parts with a Work Plan
Plant Simulation Help 10-171
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Model the Flow of Materials, Basics
Creating the Processing Stations
Create the Processing Stations
To be able to assign the operations and the stations in the work plan later on, we start out with creating
the stations which execute the operations.
• To create the class of the processing station, right-click the object Station in the folder MaterialFlow
and select Derive on the context menu.
• To move the derived Station to the folder in which we are going to create the model, hold down Shift
and drag it so that folder. Rename it to MyStation. By default Plant Simulation places derived objects
into the folder UserObjects.
• Insert it from there into the model three times. Rename the stations so that they match the names of
the operations. In our example we named them Milling, Drilling_A, Drilling_B and Packing.
As the processing stations share a number of features, we set these in their class, MyStation in our
example. The class object passes these properties on to its instances.
We will:
Creating the Processing Stations
10-172 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Define Times in the Class of the Processing Stations
• Define Set-up Behavior in the Class of the Processing Stations
• Specify the Name of the Exit Control in the Class of the Processing Stations
Back to Produce and Process Parts with a Work Plan
Define Times in the Class of the Processing Stations
We define the Processing Time and the Set-up Time in a Formula in the class of our processing stations.
• We want the stations to get the Processing Time of all parts from the work plan MyWorkPlan. This
DataTable 
  is located in the Frame in which we build the model, i.e., the root Frame. The station then
opens the subtable Operations for the respective part and gets the times in the column Processing
Time of the respective station within this subtable.
For the Processing Time we use a Method which we call from the formula. The Method is called
ProcessingTimeInFormula.
The source code looks like this:
-> time
var PartType := @.EntityType
var Station  := ?
var WorkPlan := root.myWorkPlan["Operations", PartType]
result       := WorkPlan["Processing time",Station]
Define Times in the Class of the Processing Stations
Plant Simulation Help 10-173
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• We want the stations to get the Set-up Time of all parts from the work plan MyWorkPlan. This
DataTable 
  is located in the Frame in which we build the model, i.e., the root Frame. The station
then opens the subtable Operations for the respective part and gets the times in the column Set-up
Time of the respective station within this subtable. Self identifies the station contained in the row on
the subtable.
The description above translates to this formula, which we directly type into the text box:
root.MyWorkPlan["Operations",@.EntityType]["Setup time",Self]
Back to Create the Processing Stations
Define Set-up Behavior in the Class of the Processing Stations
We
To automatically set the stations up depending on the name of a user-defined attribute of the MU:
• Select the check box Automatic.
