Back to Model the Flow of Materials, Basics
Transferring Parts from Station to Station
Transfer Parts from Station to Station
You can transfer parts from station to station in a number of ways.
When transferring parts from station to station in your simulation model, you can either:
• Use the Standard Transfer Behavior, which employs the push-block principle.
• Select an Exit Strategy, which distributes the parts to the succeeding stations according to the criteria
you set.
• Distribute Parts Among its Successors with the FlowControl.
• Move the part, when an attribute of the processing station changes by using an observer.
• Select an Exit Strategy in the Interface when parts are moving across Frames.
• Load, Unload, and Reload Parts with the TransferStation.
Transferring Parts from Station to Station
10-182 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Compare the sample models: Click the Window ribbon tab, click Start Page > Getting Started > Example
Models > Small Examples. Then, select the respective Category, the Topic, and the Example in the
dialog Examples Collection, and click Open Model.
Compare How Plant Simulation Moves Parts On
Back to Model the Flow of Materials, Basics
How Plant Simulation Moves Parts On
Plant Simulation moves the parts to be processed through the plant depending on the type of material
flow object.
Plant Simulation moves MUs on as follows:
• Moving from point-oriented to point-oriented object
When moving from a point-oriented object to another point-oriented object, the MU moves on
completely and instantaneously from booking point to booking point.
• Moving from point-oriented object to length-oriented object
When moving from a point-oriented object, such as a Source or a Station, to a Conveyor, the front of the
MU moves to the start of the Conveyor. When Station and Conveyor are not placed directly side by side,
the MU seems to hang in thin air, compare Position of the MU.
If the objects are placed directly side by side you will not notice that as the MU still seems to be located
on the predecessor.
• Moving from length-oriented object to length-oriented object
When moving from Conveyor to Conveyor, the MU moves continually on, i.e., only its front moves on
to the successor, while the remainder follows with the Speed you specified. When the transport speeds
differ, Plant Simulation uses the speed of the Conveyor, on which the Booking Point Length of the MU
is located. In the example below our MU is located on three Conveyors as it is very long.
How Plant Simulation Moves Parts On
Plant Simulation Help 10-183
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Moving from length-oriented object to point-oriented object
When moving onto a point-oriented object, such as a Station, the MU always moves completely and
instantaneously, i.e., it is then located on that object as a whole, not only its front section.
Back to Model the Flow of Materials, Basics
Use the Standard Transfer Behavior
By default Plant Simulation uses the push-block principle then transferring parts from station to station,
either along the connections you established with the Connector or by programming a Method.
An object that has processed the part, which is ready to be transferred, actively attempts to move that part
on to its successor (push). If the successor cannot receive the part at the moment, the block principle will
be activated, guaranteeing that the object ready to move the part is re-activated as soon the successor is
ready to receive the part.
The push-block principle ensures that parts are transferred using the built-in functionality of the objects
and that the stream of events is not interrupted by failures or pauses.
The standard transfer strategy is cyclic, non-blocking, meaning that the current object moves the MU to
the first non-blocked succeeding object in the flow of materials. Once the object reaches the end of the
list of successors, it continues the search at the beginning of the list and terminates the search when it
reaches the successor from the previous search.
Use the Standard Transfer Behavior
10-184 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Suppose Milling is the first station in the sequence of stations in your simulation model, Drilling_A is the
second station, Part:1 is the first mobile object that entered the flow of materials, Part:2 is the second
mobile object.
Suppose Part:1 is located on Drilling_A, and Part:2 is located on Milling and wants to move to Drilling_A.
Once Part:2 is completely processed by Milling, Part:2 notifies Drilling_A that it intends to move to it.
• When Drilling_A can receive Part:2, it notifies Part:2, and Part:2 moves from Milling to Drilling_A.
• When Drilling_A cannot receive Part:2 because it is busy, its entrance is locked, etc., Part:2 enters itself
into the forward blocking list of Drilling_A and of all other stations it intends to move to.
• When Part:1 exits Drilling_A, Drilling_A schedules an Out event for all entries, i.e., MUs, in the Forward
Blocking List, including Part:2. Afterwards Drilling_A deletes all entries from its Forward Blocking List.
• Provided Drilling_A can receive Part:2, Part:2 deletes all references to itself from all blocking lists of all
stations it entered itself into, and then moves to Drilling_A.
Use the Standard Transfer Behavior
Plant Simulation Help 10-185
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The material flow objects Station 
 , ParallelStation 
 , AssemblyStation 
 , DismantleStation 
 ,
Conveyor 
 , Sorter 
 , PlaceBuffer 
  and Buffer 
  handle the MUs that transfer onto them:
Procedure
1. For these times, which you define:
• The Processing Time is the time, which the MU remains on the object to be processed. It is the
interval between setting-up for the present type of MU and the point in time when the material
flow object moves it on to its successor.
• The Set-up Time is the time it takes to set-up the object for processing a different type of MU. An
identical name designates that MUs are of the same type and that the object does not have to set
up for this MU.
• The Recovery Time is the time which is required to set a station into a defined state before it
can start processing the next part. The recovery time is, for example, useful to model materials
handling equipment, such as a robot, which requires a certain time to insert work pieces into or
remove them from processing stations. You can also use it to model times during which a hot part
cools off.
• The Cycle Time is the time that passes between the points in time where parts can enter the
object. The cycle time is, for example, useful to model chain conveyors with a fixed chain interval
that only transport materials when a free hook is available.
Note
You do not have to define all of the times listed above for the object.
2. For failures which you define and activate.
Use the Standard Transfer Behavior
10-186 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

The material flow objects then transfer the MUs to the next object along the material flow
connections using the Exit Strategy you select.
