Selecting an Exit Strategy
Select an Exit Strategy
If you do not want to transfer the MUs using the standard transfer behavior, select an Exit Strategy.
To do so, click the Tab Exit and select another Strategy. As a rule you will use an Exit Strategy, which
distributes the flow of materials, when the sequence of operations does not uniquely designate the next
object that processes the MU. Your objective will be to make a choice that is close to optimal in some
respect, say cost or time, but not in others.
Selecting an Exit Strategy
Plant Simulation Help 10-187
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
For length-oriented objects Plant Simulation only considers the Exit Strategy when the part is moving
forward.
Note
Click Apply, to apply your new settings, and to display additional dialog items, which the selected exit
strategy requires.
• To move the part cyclically to the next successor in line, select Cyclic.
• To cyclically move the part to the successor according to the sequence of successors, which you typed
into the list, select Cyclic Sequence.
You can also set the number of the succeeding object with the attribute ExitStrategySequence.
• To move the part to the successor that has been Waiting the longest for a MU, select Least Recent
Demand.
Select an Exit Strategy
10-188 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To move the part to the successor in a linear fashion, according to the sequence of successors, which
you typed into the list, select Linear Sequence.
You can also set the number of the succeeding object with the attribute ExitStrategySequence.
• To move the part to the successor that contains the greatest number of MUs, select Maximum
Contents.
• To move the part to the successor that received the most MUs, select Maximum Number In.
• To move the part to the successor with the longest processing time, select Maximum Processing Time.
• To move the part to the successor with the highest relative occupancy, select Maximum Relative
Occupation
• To move the part to the successor with the longest set-up time, select Maximum Set-up Time.
• To move the part to the successor that contains the smallest number of MUs, select Minimum
Contents.
• To move the part to the successor that received the smallest number of MUs, select Minimum Number
In.
• To move the part to the successor with the shortest processing time, select Minimum Processing Time.
• To move the part to the successor with the lowest relative occupancy, select Minimum Relative
Occupation
• To move the part to the successor with the shortest set-up time, select Minimum Set-up Time.
• To move the part to the successor that has been Waiting the least amount of time for a MU, select Most
Recent Demand.
• To move the part to the successor according to an attribute of the MU, select MU Attribute.
You can also set the names of the user-defined or the built-in attributes, their values, and the numbers
of the successors with the attribute ExitStrategyMUAttributeList.
• To move the part to the successors according to a percentage distribution, select Percentage.
You can also set the with the attribute ExitStrategyPercentageValues.
• To move the part to the successors in a random fashion, select Random.
You can also set the with the attribute ExitStrategyPercentageValues.
• To always move the part to successor with the number 1, select Start at Successor 1.
