Unload Parts with the Transfer Station
When unloading parts, the TransferStation unloads these parts from a Container 
  or a Transporter 
 ,
which is located at the parts station, and places them onto the target station. The unloading process starts
as soon as a means of transport is ready at the parts station.
To model an unloading process, proceed like this:
• Insert a Station 
 , on to which the UnloadingStation unloads the parts from the Transporters.
• Insert a Drain 
 , which removes the parts from the plant.
• Insert a TransferStation 
 , which unloads the parts from the Transporter 
  and places them on to the
processing station namedStation. In our sample model we named it UnloadingStation. Now we tell
this station what we want it to do.
◦ We want to unload parts, so we select Unload as the Station type.
Unload Parts with the Transfer Station
10-220 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◦ Then, we have to tell it where the parts to be unloaded come from. For this we drag the Track from
the Frame ever the text box in the dialog of the UnloadingStation and drop it there.
◦ Next, we have to tell it where the means of transport, our Transporter, is located. For this we drag
the Station from the Frame ever the text box in the dialog of the UnloadingStation and drop it there.
◦ To designate the exact spot on the Track 
 , where the UnloadingStation unloads the parts from the
Transporters 
  in to the processing station Station, we enter 43 meters as the sensor position. The
UnloadingStation automatically enters this sensor into the object Track.
• To set how we want the parts to be unloaded, we click the tab Advanced Attributes. We want to unload
all blocks of parts in blocks of 1.
• Insert a Method, enter deleteMovables is the source code, and name the Method reset.
• Insert an EventController into the Frame, and run the simulation.
You can then change a number of settings, such as the dimensions of the Transporters and the
Containers, the block size, the number of blocks, etc. and watch how these changes affect the
simulation model.
